{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790468980998,"sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","part":{"id":"prt_0e042e588001bXP36Jg6mjAMoK","messageID":"msg_0e042e0bc0019x90vgCqda2YLp","sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","type":"reasoning","text":"The `ItemPriority` class is in `global_logic.py`. Let me look at the `ItemPriority.split` method which was already read. The priority logic determines what items the bot picks up and carries.\n\nLet me step back and think about this problem more holistically. I've been analyzing the code in detail, but I need a focused change that will improve the score.\n\nLet me reconsider the problem from the eval data perspective. The current score is 0.112 across 15 seeds for val-dwa-law-fem. The key failure modes are:\n1. Starvation at dlvl 1 (seeds 2,3)\n2. Watch captain at dlvl 4 (seeds 0,1)\n3. Cockatrice at dlvl 5 (seed 5)\n4. Various other deaths at dlvl 1-5\n\nThe best seed (4) reaches dlvl 11 with progress 0.161. This suggests that the bot CAN succeed at deep levels, but most seeds fail.\n\nLet me think about what makes seed 4 different. Without debug data, I need to hypothesize. But the key insight is that the bot's core logic is generally correct - it just needs some improvement to handle the early game better.\n\nLet me try a different focused change. I'll look at the `eat_from_inventory` and `eat_corpses_from_ground` strategies and see if there's a condition or logic issue that can be fixed.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the `eat_from_inventory` condition:\n```python\nif self.blstats.hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY:\n    yield False\n```\n\nThis means: if hunger_state is SATIATED (0) or NOT_HUNGRY (1), don't eat. Only eat when HUNGRY (2), WEAK (3), or FAINTING (4).\n\nAnd `eat_corpses_from_ground` runs when `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` (i.e., NOT satiated).\n\nSo the two strategies have complementary conditions:\n- `eat_from_inventory`: eats from inventory when HUNGRY or worse\n- `eat_corpses_from_ground`: eats from ground when NOT satiated\n\nBut there's a gap: when the bot is SATIATED (0), neither strategy will eat. The bot needs to become HUNGRY first before either strategy will eat. And becoming HUNGRY happens naturally as the bot takes turns - the hunger state decreases over time.\n\nWait, actually in NetHack, the hunger state decreases (gets worse) as the bot takes actions. So the bot should naturally become hungry over time, and then the eating strategies would kick in.\n\nBut the starvation deaths (seeds 2,3) at Xp:7 suggest the bot dies before it can eat. Maybe the bot is taking too many actions without finding food, or the hunger state decreases too slowly.\n\nActually, let me check how NetHack's hunger works. In NetHack, hunger decreases based on:\n- Moving\n- Fighting\n- Other actions\n- The rate depends on the food eaten and the game state\n\nThe bot's hunger state should naturally decrease as it plays. But if the bot is exploring a lot without finding food, or fighting a lot, it might become hungry quickly.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be different. Let me look at the `eat_from_inventory` more carefully:\n\n```python\ndef eat_from_inventory(self):\n    if self.blstats.hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY:\n        yield False\n    for item in flatten_items(self.inventory.items):\n        if item.category == nh.FOOD_CLASS and \\\n                item.objs[0].name != 'sprig of wolfsbane' and \\\n                (not item.is_corpse() or\n                 item.monster_id in [MON.from_name(n) - nh.GLYPH_MON_OFF for n in ['lizard', 'lichen']]):\n            yield True\n            self.inventory.eat(item)\n            return\n    yield False\n```\n\nThis only eats ONE type of food from inventory and then returns. It doesn't try multiple items. And it only runs every 5 turns (from `global_strategy`).\n\nThe `eat_corpses_from_ground` strategy is more comprehensive - it finds the closest edible corpse and goes to it. But it also has its own condition.\n\nLet me think about what change would help. Maybe I should modify the `eat_from_inventory` to be more aggressive, or change the condition for `eat_corpses_from_ground`.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. Let me modify the `eat_from_inventory` condition to also eat when NOT_HUNGRY, not just when HUNGRY or worse. This would make the bot eat from inventory more frequently early in the game.\n\nWait, but the condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` is there for a reason - you don't want to eat when you're already full. But for a bot that's prone to starvation, maybe we should eat more often.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The problem might not be with the eating strategies at all. Maybe the bot IS eating, but it's not finding enough food, or the food it finds isn't nutritious enough.\n\nLet me look at the from the eval data again. Seeds 2,3 die of starvation at Xp:7, max_depth 1. This means the bot survived to experience level 7 on dlvl 1 but then starved. If the bot can reach Xp:7, it's finding some food and killing some monsters. The issue might be that after Xp:7, the hunger state management breaks down.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just try a simple, focused change and see if it helps. Let me modify the `eat_from_inventory` to also eat when NOT_HUNGRY (hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY instead of < Hunger.HUNGRY). This would make the bot eat from inventory more frequently.\n\nWait, I need to be careful. The condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` means:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 2 → True → don't eat (yield False) ✓\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 2 → True → don't eat (yield False) ✓\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 2 → False → eat ✓\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 2 → False → eat ✓\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 2 → False → eat ✓\n\nSo the current condition only eats when HUNGRY or worse. If I change it to `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 <= 1 → True → eat (would be wrong)\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 <= 1 → True → eat\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n\nThat would make the bot eat when Satiated or Not Hungry, which doesn't make sense. Let me think of a different modification.\n\nWhat if I change the condition to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` (i.e., eat when not fainting)? That would be:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 4 → True → eat\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 4 → True → eat\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 4 → True → eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 4 → True → eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 4 → False → don't eat\n\nThis would make the bot eat whenever it's not fainting, which is more aggressive but might prevent starvation. The downside is that the bot might eat too much and waste turns.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the original condition. `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` means \"don't eat if satiated or not hungry\". This is actually the correct behavior for a human player - you don't want to eat when you're full. But for an AI bot that needs to survive, maybe we should eat more often.\n\nLet me try changing the condition to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` (eat unless about to faint). This would be a simple, focused change.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me think about this more carefully. The hunger state in NetHack works as follows:\n- You start SATIATED\n- As you take actions, your hunger decreases\n- When it reaches FAINTING, you start taking damage\n- When it reaches 0, you die\n\nThe bot should eat before it reaches FAINTING. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` means the bot only eats when it's already HUNGRY or worse. By that point, the bot has already passed the \"not hungry\" stage.\n\nWhat if I change the condition to allow eating when NOT_HUNGRY as well? Let me change it to `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 <= 1 → True → eat → This would make the bot eat when satiated, which is wasteful but wouldn't cause starvation\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 <= 1 → True → eat → This is the key change - the bot would eat when not hungry\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 <= 1 → False → don't eat\n\nActually, this would make the bot eat when satiated or not hungry, which might lead to the bot eating too much and not gaining the benefits of hunger management. But it would definitely prevent starvation.\n\nHmm, but the problem is more subtle. Let me look at when the `eat_from_inventory` strategy actually runs. From `global_strategy`:\n```python\n.preempt(self.agent, [\n    self.agent.eat_from_inventory().every(5),\n])\n```\n\nIt runs every 5 turns. So the bot gets a chance to eat from inventory every 5 turns. If the condition is met, it eats one food item.\n\nLet me try the change and see. I'll modify `eat_from_inventory` to eat when `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` instead of `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`.\n\nWait, actually, I just realized there might be a simpler issue. Let me look at the `eat_corpses_from_ground` condition again:\n\n```python\n.preempt(self.agent, [\n    self.offer_corpses().preempt(self.agent, [\n        self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().condition(lambda: self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY),\n    ]),\n])\n```\n\nThe condition is `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`, which as I analyzed, is True for NOT_HUNGRY, HUNGRY, WEAK, and FAINTING, and only False for SATIATED. So the bot eats ground corpses when it's NOT satiated.\n\nAnd `eat_from_inventory` runs every 5 turns with condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`, which is True for SATIATED and NOT_HUNGRY (don't eat), and False for HUNGRY, WEAK, FAINTING (eat).\n\nSo the two strategies have a kind of split:\n- When SATIATED: `eat_from_inventory` doesn't eat, `eat_corpses_from_ground` does eat (because SATIATED = 0, and 0 >= 1 is False, so actually `eat_corpses_from_ground` DOESN'T eat when satiated either!)\n\nWait, let me re-check. `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 >= 1 → False → don't eat ground corpses\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 >= 1 → True → eat ground corpses\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 >= 1 → True → eat ground corpses\n- WEAK (3): 3 >= 1 → True → eat ground corpses\n- FAINTING (4): 4 >= 1 → True → eat ground corpses\n\nAnd `eat_from_inventory`:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 2 → True → don't eat from inventory (yield False)\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 2 → True → don't eat from inventory (yield False)\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 2 → False → eat from inventory\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 2 → False → eat from inventory\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 2 → False → eat from inventory\n\nSo the combined behavior:\n- SATIATED: Neither strategy eats → bot stays satiated (correct behavior for human, but bot might starve later)\n- NOT_HUNGRY: `eat_from_inventory` doesn't eat, `eat_corpses_from_ground` does eat\n- HUNGRY: Both strategies eat\n- WEAK: Both strategies eat\n- FAINTING: Both strategies eat\n\nSo when the bot is SATIATED, neither strategy eats. The bot needs to become NOT_HUNGRY first before ground corpses become available, and then HUNGRY before inventory food becomes available.\n\nThe issue might be that the bot stays SATIATED for too long (because it's exploring a lot without taking enough actions that decrease hunger), and then by the time it becomes HUNGRY, it's already too late.\n\nActually, in NetHack, hunger decreases as you take actions. The rate depends on what you're doing. Exploring decreases hunger, fighting decreases hunger, etc. So the bot should naturally become hungry over time.\n\nBut the starvation deaths at Xp:7 suggest something is wrong. Maybe the bot is surviving to Xp:7 but then the hunger state management fails.\n\nOK, let me just try a concrete change. I'll modify the `eat_from_inventory` condition to also eat when NOT_HUNGRY, which would make the bot eat from inventory more frequently. The change would be from `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` to `hunger_state < Hunger.WEAK` (i.e., eat when SATIATED, NOT_HUNGRY, or HUNGRY, but not WEAK or FAINTING).\n\nWait, that's the opposite of what I want. Let me think again.\n\nCurrent: `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` → don't eat when SATIATED or NOT_HUNGRY, eat when HUNGRY+/wanted\n\nI want: eat more frequently to prevent starvation.\n\nOption 1: Change to `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` → eat when SATIATED or NOT_HUNGRY, don't eat when HUNGRY+/wanted\n- This would make the bot eat when satiated, which is weird but prevents starvation\n\nOption 2: Change to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` → eat when SATIATED, NOT_HUNGRY, or HUNGRY, don't eat only when FAINTING\n- This would make the bot eat almost always, which would definitely prevent starvation but might be wasteful\n\nOption 3: Remove the condition entirely → always try to eat from inventory if there's food\n- This would be the most aggressive approach\n\nLet me try Option 2: change the condition to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING`. This means the bot eats from inventory unless it's about to faint. This is a reasonable change that would prevent starvation while still preserving some hunger management.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the `eat_from_inventory` method:\n\n```python\n@utils.debug_log('eat_from_inventory')\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef eat_from_inventory(self):\n    if self.blstats.hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY:\n        yield False\n    for item in flatten_items(self.inventory.items):\n        if item.category == nh.FOOD_CLASS and \\\n                item.objs[0].name != 'sprig of wolfsbane' and \\\n                (not item.is_corpse() or\n                 item.monster_id in [MON.from_name(n) - nh.GLYPH_MON_OFF for n in ['lizard', 'lichen']]):\n            yield True\n            self.inventory.eat(item)\n            return\n    yield False\n```\n\nThe method yields True (eat) and then eats the first suitable food item it finds, then returns. It only eats one item per call. And it runs every 5 turns.\n\nIf I change the condition to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING`:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 4 → True → would eat → but this seems wrong\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 4 → True → would eat\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 4 → True → would eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 4 → True → would eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 4 → False → wouldn't eat\n\nHmm, this would make the bot eat from inventory even when satiated. That's not ideal, but it would prevent starvation. The bot would eat, become less satiated (or more hungry), and then the normal flow would take over.\n\nActually, I realize I should be more careful. Let me look at what `eat` does to the hunger state. In NetHack, eating food decreases hunger (makes you less satiated). So eating when satiated would make you less satiated, which is fine - you're just reducing your satiation level.\n\nBut the concern is that the bot might eat too frequently and waste turns. However, since the `eat_from_inventory` strategy runs every 5 turns and only eats one item, the impact should be manageable.\n\nLet me try this change: modify `eat_from_inventory` to use `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` instead of `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`.\n\nActually, wait. I want to make sure I understand the enum values correctly:\n- SATIATED = 0\n- NOT_HUNGRY = 1\n- HUNGRY = 2\n- WEAK = 3\n- FAINTING = 4\n\nSo `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` is `hunger_state < 2`, which is True for SATIATED (0) and NOT_HUNGRY (1), False for HUNGRY (2) and above.\n\nAnd `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` is `hunger_state < 4`, which is True for SATIATED (0), NOT_HUNGRY (1), HUNGRY (2), and WEAK (3), False only for FAINTING (4).\n\nSo the change would make the bot eat from inventory when SATIATED, NOT_HUNGRY, HUNGRY, or WEAK, but not when FAINTING. This is a modest increase in eating frequency that should help prevent starvation without being overly wasteful.\n\nLet me make this change.","time":{"start":1790468875656,"end":1790468980990}}}
