it causes monsters to skip their turn when you're engraving it. The bot does have elbereth support in `fight2` and `elbereth_action`, but it might not be engraving it early enough.\n\nLooking at the `elbereth_action` function:\n```python\ndef elbereth_action(agent, monsters):\n    if agent.inventory.engraving_below_me.lower() == 'elbereth':\n        return []\n    if not agent.can_engrave():\n        return []\n    adj_monsters_count = 0\n    ...\n    player_hp_ratio = (agent.blstats.hitpoints / agent.blstats.max_hitpoints) ** 0.5\n    if agent.blstats.hitpoints < 30 and adj_monsters_count > 0:\n        return [(-15 + 20 * adj_monsters_count * (1 - player_hp_ratio), ('elbereth',))]\n    return []\n```\n\nElbereth is only recommended when `hitpoints < 30`. By that point, the bot might already be in trouble. If the bot engraved Elbereth earlier (when it first gets a chance), it would have better survival throughout the early game.\n\nBut the `current_strategy` milestones might not allow engraving Elbereth early. The bot stays on `BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL` until `exp >= 8`, and only then can it proceed to other strategies. And the `elbereth_action` is only called from `get_available_actions`, which is called from `get_priorities`, which is called from `fight2`, which is preempted by many other strategies.\n\nLet me try a different change. What if I modify the `current_strategy` to allow engraving Elbereth earlier? Or what if I add a condition to engrave Elbereth when the bot first has the opportunity?\n\nActually, let me try the most impactful change I can think of: modify the `global_strategy` to engraE Elbereth as a early-game strategy, before the main exploration starts. Elbereth is universally useful for survival.\n\nBut wait, the `current_strategy` is complex and changing it might have unintended consequences. Let me look at a simpler change.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the code flow. The `global_strategy` is:\n```python\ndef global_strategy(self):\n    return (\n        self.current_strategy().repeat()\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.solve_sokoban_strategy()\n            .condition(lambda: self.milestone == Milestone.SOLVE_SOKOBAN and\n                        self.agent.current_level().dungeon_number == Level.SOKOBAN)\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.offer_corpses().preempt(self.agent, [\n                self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().condition(lambda: self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY),\n            ]),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.cure_disease().every(5),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().condition(lambda: self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY),\n            self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().every(5).condition(lambda: self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY),\n            self.agent.eat_from_inventory().every(5),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.follow_guard(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.fight2(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.engulfed_fight(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.emergency_strategy(),\n        ])\n    )\n```\n\nThe `fight2` strategy is #8 in the preemption chain. It only runs when all higher-priority strategies don't apply. The strategies above it handle:\n1. Sokoban\n2. Offering/corpores and eating corpses from ground\n3. Unexpected states (blind, confused, etc.)\n4. Disease cure every 5 turns\n5. More eating (corpses and inventory) every 5 turns\n6. Follow guard (gold dropping)\n7. Then fight2\n\nThe issue is that `fight2` only gets to run when the bot is not hungry, not in an unexpected state, not diseased, not eating, not following a guard, and not engulfed. This means `fight2` should get plenty of chances when the bot is in a normal state.\n\nBut the problem might be that the bot is dying before it can get to a normal state. The starvation deaths (seeds 2,3) happen at dlvl 1, Xp:7. If the bot is eating enough, it shouldn't starve. But the eating strategies might not be working effectively.\n\nLet me try modifying the `global_strategy` to give `fight2` a higher priority, or to reduce the number of preemptions that block `fight2`.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Let me modify the `current_strategy` to engrave Elbereth at the start of the game. Elbereth is so powerful that engraving it early should significantly improve survival.\n\nLooking at the `current_strategy`:\n```python\nif self.milestone == Milestone.BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL:\n    condition = lambda: self.agent.blstats.experience_level >= 8\n    level = (Level.DUNGEONS_OF_DOOM, 1)\n```\n\nThe bot stays on dlvl 1 until exp >= 8. During this time, the bot is exploring and fighting. If I add an elbereth engraving condition early in the `BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL` milestone, the bot could engrave Elbereth early and have better survival.\n\nBut I need to be careful about how and when to engrave. The `engrave` method requires \"What do you want to write with?\" prompt, and the bot needs a tool to write with (usually a finger or a candle).\n\nActually, let me try a simpler change. Let me look at the `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy` and see if there's an issue there. This strategy handles blind, confused, stun, hallu, and polymorph. If the bot is getting these status effects frequently, it could explain the low scores.\n\nBut I don't have evidence that this is the issue.\n\nOK, let me just try a concrete, focused change and measure its effect. Let me modify the `eat_corpses_from_ground` condition to be less restrictive. Currently it's `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`, which means it only eats when NOT satiated. Let me change it to `hunger_state != Hunger.SATIATED` (i.e., eat when not satiated, which includes NOT_HUNGRY, HUNGRY, WEAK, and FAINTING).\n\nWait, that's the same thing. `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` is True for NOT_HUNGRY (1), HUNGRY (2), WEAK (3), FAINTING (4), and False for SATIATED (0). So `hunger_state != Hunger.SATIATED` would be the same condition.\n\nHmm, let me think of something else.\n\nActually, let me look at the `offer_corpses` strategy. It offers corpses on altars to get gifts from the god. The `global_strategy` has:\n```python\n.preempt(self.agent, [\n    self.offer_corpses().preempt(self.agent, [\n        self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().condition(lambda: self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY),\n    ]),\n])\n```\n\nThe `offer_corpses` strategy runs first, and if it doesn't apply (returns False), then `eat_corpses_from_ground` runs. But `offer_corpses` might be preempting `eat_corpses_from_ground` even when the bot is hungry and should eat.\n\nActually, looking at the `preempt` method, the first strategy (offer_corpses) has priority. If it yields False (doesn't apply), then the second strategy (eat_corpses_from_ground) gets a chance. If it yields True, the second strategy doesn't run. This seems correct.\n\nBut wait, `offer_corpses` might yield True even when there are no altars available, or when the bot shouldn't be offering. Let me look at the `offer_corpses` strategy more carefully.\n\nFrom `global_logic.py`:\n```python\n@utils.debug_log('offer_corpses')\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef offer_corpses(self):\n    self.item_priority._take_sacrificial_corpses = False\n\n    if self._got_artifact:\n        yield False\n\n    altars = [p for p, alignment in self.agent.current_level().altars.items()\n              if alignment == self.agent.character.alignment]\n\n    if not altars:\n        yield False\n\n    dis = self.agent.bfs()\n    altars = [(y, x) for y, x in altars if dis[y, x] != -1]\n\n    if not altars:\n        yield False\n\n    self.item_priority._take_sacrificial_corpses = True\n\n    if not any((self.can_sacrify(item) for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items))):\n        yield False\n\n    yield True\n\n    y, x = min(altars, key=lambda p: dis[p])\n    self.agent.go_to(y, x)\n    with self.agent.panic_if_position_changes():\n        while 1:\n            for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items):\n                if self.can_sacrify(item):\n                    with self.agent.atom_operation():\n                        item = self.agent.inventory.move_to_inventory(item)\n                        assert self.can_sacrify(item)\n                        self.agent.step(A.Command.OFFER)\n                        while ('There is ' in self.agent.message or 'There are ' in self.agent.message) and \\\n                                ('sacrifice it?' in self.agent.message or 'sacrifice one?' in self.agent.message):\n                            self.agent.type_text('n')\n                        assert 'What do you want to sacrifice?' in self.agent.message, self.agent.message\n                        self.agent.type_text(self.agent.inventory.items.get_letter(item))\n                        if 'Nothing happens.' in self.agent.message:\n                            self.agent.inventory.call_item(item, 'old')\n                            return\n                        if 'Use my gift wisely' in self.agent.message:\n                            self._got_artifact = True\n                            self.agent.inventory.get_items_below_me()\n                            return\n                        if 'So this is how you repay loyalty?' in self.agent.message:\n                            raise AgentPanic('pet sacrified')\n                        assert 'Your sacrifice is consumed in a flash of light' in self.agent.message or \\\n                            'Your sacrifice is consumed in a burst of flame' in self.agent.message or \\\n                            ('The blood covers the altar!' in self.agent.message and \\\n                             'You have summoned ' in self.agent.message), \\\n                            self.agent.message\n                        break\n            else:\n                break\n```\n\nThe `offer_corpses` strategy offers a corpse on an altar to get a gift from the god. It runs when there are aligned altars and sacrificeable corpses. If the bot is offers corpses and gets gifts, this could be very beneficial. But if the bot is just offering corpses without getting useful gifts, it's wasting time.\n\nFor a lawful valkyrie, the aligned altar would be lawful. The bot would offer lawful corpses (human, dwarf, elf, gnome) to get lawful gifts. This might not be the most beneficial strategy for a valkyrie, but it's not harmful either.\n\nOK, I've been analyzing for too long. Let me just try a concrete change and see if it helps. Let me modify the `global_strategy` to give `fight2` higher priority by moving it earlier in the chain, or by reducing the number of preemptions before it.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the problem statement one more time:\n\n\"Make ONE focused change that raises its score.\"\n\nAnd: \"Keep the make_agent() → reset() / act() contract intact and make sure the code imports cleanly.\"\n\nAnd: \"Don't game the score: no branching on seed fingerprints, no exploiting scorer/NLE quirks — those don't generalize.\"\n\nSo I need to make a genuine improvement to the bot's gameplay, not a hack.\n\nLet me try one more thing. Let me look at the `emergency_strategy` method:","time":{"start":1790469136976,"end":1790469240093}}}
