{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790469451567,"sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","part":{"id":"prt_0e048afe5001sZCbJuEZ529poi","messageID":"msg_0e048ab15001SeFrQd7bkgvyjT","sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","type":"reasoning","text":"Good, the stairs_down code I added and removed is gone. The file is back to its original state.\n\nLet me take a fresh look at the problem. The current score is 0.112 for val-dwa-law-fem across 15 seeds. The key failure modes are:\n\n1. Seeds 2,3: starve at dlvl 1, Xp:7\n2. Seeds 0,1: reach dlvl 4, Xp:9, die to watch captain\n3. Seed 5: petrified by cockatrice at dlvl 5\n4. Other seeds: various deaths at dlvl 1-5\n\nThe best seed (4) reaches dlvl 11 with progress 0.161.\n\nLet me think about what single change would most improve the score. I need a \"focused\" change - one coherent idea.\n\nKey observations:\n- The bot is val-dwa-law-fem (Valkyrie, Dwarf, Lawful, Female)\n- Dwarves have innate poison and spell resistance\n- Valkyries are warrior-like, proficient with weapons\n- Lawful alignment conducts conduct\n\nThe bot's core loop is:\n1. `explore1` - explore the current level\n2. `fight2` - combat when monsters are visible\n3. `global_strategy` - manage milestones, eating, item priority\n\nThe bot gets stuck at dlvl 1 because it can't survive or progress. The most common failure is starvation.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Instead of modifying the exploration or combat logic, let me modify the `eat_from_inventory` strategy to be more robust. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` means the bot only eats from inventory when it's already HUNGRY or worse. By changing this to allow eating when NOT_HUNGRY as well, the bot would eat more frequently and prevent starvation.\n\nBut my earlier attempt at this was too aggressive (eating when SATIATED caused issues). Let me try a more nuanced change.\n\nActually, let me look at the `eat_from_inventory` method again:\n\n```python\ndef eat_from_inventory(self):\n    if self.blstats.hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY:\n        yield False\n    for item in flatten_items(self.inventory.items):\n        if item.category == nh.FOOD_CLASS and \\\n                item.objs[0].name != 'sprig of wolfsbane' and \\\n                (not item.is_corpse() or\n                 item.monster_id in [MON.from_name(n) - nh.GLYPH_MON_OFF for n in ['lizard', 'lichen']]):\n            yield True\n            self.inventory.eat(item)\n            return\n    yield False\n```\n\nThe method eats the FIRST suitable food item it finds and then returns. It only eats one item per call. And it runs every 5 turns.\n\nWhat if I change the condition from `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` to `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`? This would make the bot eat from inventory when SATIATED (0 <= 1 = True) or NOT_HUNGRY (1 <= 1 = True), but not when HUNGRY (2 <= 1 = False) or worse.\n\nWait, that's the opposite of what I want. I want the bot to eat MORE, not less.\n\nLet me think again. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` is True for SATIATED and NOT_HUNGRY, meaning \"don't eat\". The bot only eats when `hunger_state >= Hunger.HUNGRY`, i.e., HUNGRY, WEAK, or FAINTING.\n\nWhat if I change the condition to `hunger_state < Hunger.WEAK`? This would be True for SATIATED (0 < 3) and NOT_HUNGRY (1 < 3), False for HUNGRY (2 < 3? No, 2 < 3 is True) and WEAK (3 < 3 is False). Wait, let me recalculate:\n\n`hunger_state < Hunger.WEAK` where WEAK = 3:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 3 → True → don't eat\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 3 → True → don't eat\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 3 → True → don't eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 3 → False → eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 3 → False → eat\n\nThis would make the bot eat only when WEAK or FAINTING, which is less frequent than the current behavior (currently eats when HUNGRY+, so HUNGRY, WEAK, FAINTING). This would be worse, not better.\n\nWhat about `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING` where FAINTING = 4:\n- SATIATED (0): 0 < 4 → True → don't eat\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1): 1 < 4 → True → don't eat\n- HUNGRY (2): 2 < 4 → True → don't eat\n- WEAK (3): 3 < 4 → True → don't eat\n- FAINTING (4): 4 < 4 → False → eat\n\nThis would make the bot eat ONLY when FAINTING, which is much less frequent. Bad.\n\nOK, so changing the condition to allow eating at different hunger states doesn't easily give me what I want. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` means \"don't eat when satiated or not hungry, eat when hungry or worse.\" This is actually a reasonable restriction - you don't want to eat when you're already full.\n\nThe issue might not be with the `eat_from_inventory` condition at all. Maybe the bot IS eating (from corpses on the ground), but the issue is that there aren't enough corpses, or the corpses it finds aren't nutritious enough, or the `_is_corpse_editable` check is too restrictive.\n\nLet me look at the `_is_corpse_editable` check for dwarves more carefully. The check at line 1249-1250:\n```python\nif self.character.race != Character.ORC and permonst.mflags1 & MON.M1_POIS != 0:\n    return False\n```\n\nThis rejects poisonous corpses for all non-orc races. But dwarves have innate poison resistance in NetHack. This is a general restriction that affects dwarves disproportionately since they're more likely to encounter and kill poisonous monsters.\n\nSimilarly, the acid check at line 1253:\n```python\nif permonst.mflags1 & MON.M1_ACID != 0:\n    return False\n```\n\nThis unconditionally rejects acidous corpses. But again, dwarves might have some resistance.\n\nAnd the stun check at line 1272-1273:\n```python\nif monster_id in [MON.id_from_name(name) for name in ['bat', 'giant bat']]:\n    return False\n```\n\nBats are common early-game monsters, and their corpses provide nutrition. Barring them from being eaten limits food options.\n\nAnd the petrification check at line 1285-1286:\n```python\nif ord(permonst.mlet) == MON.S_COCKATRICE or monster_id == MON.id_from_name('Medusa'):\n    return False\n```\n\nThis is correct for survival - eating cockatrice corpses causes petrification. But it also limits food options.\n\nAnd the aggravate monster check at line 1276-1278:\n```python\nif monster_id in [MON.id_from_name(name) for name in ['dog', 'little dog', 'large dog',\n                                                            'kitten', 'housecat', 'large cat']]:\n    return False\n```\n\nThese are pets. Eating pets is generally discouraged in NetHack (it conducts alignment), but for survival, it's sometimes necessary.\n\nAnd the cannibalism check at line 1293-1303:\n```python\nrace_flag = {\n    Character.HUMAN: MON.M2_HUMAN,\n    Character.DWARF: MON.M2_DWARF,\n    Character.ELF: MON.M2_ELF,\n    Character.GNOME: MON.M2_GNOME,\n    Character.ORC: 0,\n}[self.character.race]\nif permonst.mflags2 & race_flag:\n    return False\n```\n\nFor a dwarf, this rejects corpses with the DWARF flag. This would prevent dwarves from eating dwarf corpses, which makes sense (cannibalism conduct for lawful dwarves perhaps?). But it also limits food options.\n\nOK, I think the `_is_corpse_editable` checks are too restrictive for the dwarf valkyrie. But the problem says \"Prefer general NetHack improvements over tricks tuned to these particular dungeons.\" So I should make a general improvement, not one specifically for dwarves.\n\nHowever, modifying the `_is_corpse_editable` to be less restrictive about poison/acid might be a general improvement that helps many races, not just dwarves. In NetHack, eating poisonous corpses can be dangerous, but the game already has mechanics for dealing with poison (golden amulet of life saving, poison resistance intrinsics, etc.). The current check might be too conservative.\n\nActually, let me take a completely different approach. Let me look at what the `check_terrain` function does and if there's a way to improve the bot's early game by better terrain interaction.\n\nThe `check_terrain` function (in agent.py line 669-676):\n```python\ndef check_terrain(self, force):\n    if force or self._last_terrain_check is None or self.blstats.time - self._last_terrain_check > 50:\n        self._last_terrain_check = self.blstats.time\n        with self.atom_operation():\n            self.type_text('#te')\n            self.step(A.MiscAction.MORE, iter('b'))\n            self.update_level()\n            self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n```\n\nThis uses the `#te` command to examine the terrain and search for items. It's called periodically (every 50 turns) from `update_state`. This helps the bot identify items and terrain, which is important for finding food and weapons.\n\nBut this is already in the code and functioning. The issue might be that the bot isn't interpreting the `#te` output correctly, or it's not using the information effectively.\n\nOK, I've been going back and forth for too long. Let me just make a focused, reasonable change and test it. Here's my plan:\n\n**Change**: Modify the `eat_from_inventory` strategy to eat when `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` instead of `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`. \n\nWait, I already tried this direction and it didn't work well. Let me think differently.\n\nActually, let me try modifying the `_is_corpse_editable` to be less restrictive about poison. Specifically, I'll add a check for dwarf poison resistance. But the problem says not to tune to particular identities...\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. Let me modify the `global_strategy` to give the `fight2` strategy more opportunities by reducing some preemptions. Specifically, I'll reduce the frequency or strength of the `eat_corpses_from_ground` preemption, so `fight2` gets more chances to run.\n\nActually, let me look at this from a completely different angle. What if the issue is that the bot is not using the `>` (down staircase) and `<` (up staircase) keys to explore? The `explore1` function should handle this, but maybe there's a bug or suboptimal behavior.\n\nLet me look at the `explore_stairs` strategy:\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef explore_stairs(self, go_to_strategy, **kwargs):\n    unexplored_stairs = self.get_unexplored_stairs(**kwargs)\n    if len(unexplored_stairs) == 0:\n        yield False\n    yield True\n\n    y, x = list(unexplored_stairs)[self.agent.rng.randint(0, len(unexplored_stairs))]\n    glyph = self.agent.current_level().objects[y, x]\n    dir = '>' if glyph in G.STAIR_DOWN else '<' if glyph in G.STAIR_UP else ''\n    assert dir, glyph  # TODO: portals\n\n    go_to_strategy(y, x).run()\n    assert (self.agent.blstats.y, self.agent.blstats.x) == (y, x)\n    while self.agent.has_pet:\n        if utils.any_in(self.agent.glyphs[max(self.agent.blstats.y - 1, 0) : self.agent.blstats.y + 2,\n                                          max(self.agent.blstats.x - 1, 0) : self.agent.blstats.x + 2],\n                        G.PETS):\n            break\n        self.agent.move('.')\n    self.agent.move(dir)\n```\n\nThis strategy explores unexplored stairs. But it's preempted by other strategies in the `global_strategy` chain. Let me check when it's actually called.\n\nLooking at `current_strategy`:\n```python\nif self.milestone == Milestone.BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL:\n    condition = lambda: self.agent.blstats.experience_level >= 8\n    level = (Level.DUNGEONS_OF_DOOM, 1)\n```\n\nAnd then later:\n```python\n(\n    self.agent.exploration.go_to_level_strategy(*level, go_to_strategy, exploration_strategy(None))\n    .before(exploration_strategy(None))#.before(self.agent.exploration.patrol())\n    .preempt(self.agent, [\n        exploration_strategy(0),\n        exploration_strategy(None).until(\n            self.agent, lambda: self.agent.blstats.hitpoints >= 0.8 * self.agent.blstats.max_hitpoints)\n    ])\n    .preempt(self.agent, [\n        self.agent.exploration.explore_stairs(go_to_strategy, all=True).condition(explore_stairs_condition),\n    ])\n    .until(self.agent, condition)\n).run()\n```\n\nSo `explore_stairs` is preempted and only runs under certain conditions. The `explore1` function (from `exploration_logic.py`) is the main exploration driver, not `explore_stairs`.\n\nOK, I'm going to try a specific, focused change now. Let me modify the `explore1` function's `to_search_func` to prioritize stairs down. I'll add a priority boost for stairs down tiles, similar to what I tried before but with a more moderate value and possibly with conditions.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different hypothesis. What if the bot's issue is that it's not reading the `mmaps` (monster maps) correctly, or not using the `#` (examine) command effectively? \n\nNo, let me just try the stairs-down priority change with a very small value and see what happens. I'll add it to the `to_search_func` with priority 10 (instead of 100 or 1000).\n\nWait, I already tried this and it didn't work well. The issue might be that the bot needs more than just a priority boost - it needs a fundamental change in how it explores and descends.\n\nLet me try one more thing. Let me modify the `global_strategy` to reorder the preemptions, giving `fight2` higher priority. Currently `fight2` is #8, after many other strategies. If I move it earlier, the bot would combat more frequently, which might help it gain experience and progress deeper.\n\nBut I need to be careful - reducing preemptions might cause other issues (like the bot not eating or handling status effects).\n\nActually, let me try the simplest possible change: modify the `global_strategy` to move `fight2` earlier in the preemption chain, before the eating strategies. This would make the bot fight more and eat less frequently, which might help it gain experience faster.\n\nBut this could cause the bot to die more often from not eating... \n\nOK, let me just try a concrete change and test it. I'll move `fight2` earlier in the `global_strategy` chain.","time":{"start":1790469255141,"end":1790469451560}}}
