wering the exp threshold might be too specific.\n\nOK, let me try modifying the `global_strategy` to move `fight2` before the eating strategies. This would make the bot fight more aggressively early on, which could help it gain experience faster. The eating strategies would still run, but `fight2` would get first crack at actions when combat is available.\n\nActually wait, I need to understand the preemption better. The `preempt` method works by having inner strategies that can raise `AgentChangeStrategy` to take over. The outer strategy only runs if the inner ones yield or fail.\n\nLooking at the chain structure:\n```\ncurrent_strategy().repeat()\n  .preempt(solve_sokoban_strategy())  # outer: current_strategy, inner: solve_sokoban\n  .preempt(offer_corpses().preempt(eat_corpses_from_ground()))  # outer: offer_corpses, inner: eat_corpses_from_ground\n  .preempt(wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy())\n  .preempt(cure_disease().every(5))\n  .preempt(eat_corpses_from_ground().every(5).condition(...), eat_from_inventory().every(5))  # multiple inner strategies\n  .preempt(follow_guard())\n  .preempt(fight2())  # outermost: fight2\n  .preempt(engulfed_fight())\n  .preempt(emergency_strategy())\n```\n\nActually, I'm not sure about the exact nesting. Let me re-read the `preempt` method in `strategy.py`.\n\nFrom `strategy.py`:\n```python\ndef preempt(self, agent, strategies, continue_after_preemption=True):\n    \"\"\" Specify other strategies that may preempt the strategy \"\"\"\n    def f(self=self, agent=agent, strategies=strategies):\n        gen = self.strategy()\n        condition_passed = False\n        with agent.disallow_step_calling():\n            condition_passed = next(gen)\n\n        if not condition_passed:\n            yield False\n        yield True\n\n        assert not agent._no_step_calls\n\n        def f2():\n            try:\n                next(gen)\n                assert 0, gen\n            except StopIteration as e:\n                return e.value\n\n        return agent.preempt(strategies, self, first_func=f2, continue_after_preemption=continue_after_preemption)\n\n    return Strategy(f, {'strategy': self.config, 'preempt': [s.config for s in strategies]})\n```\n\nThe `preempt` method wraps the current strategy with a preemption check. The `agent.preempt(strategies, self, first_func=f2, ...)` call is the key - it tells the agent to run the given strategies, with the current strategy as a fallback.\n\nThe way it works is:\n1. The current strategy runs first (via `next(gen)`)\n2. If the strategy's condition passes, the preempted strategies run\n3. If the strategy's condition fails, the preempted strategies don't run (or the first_func runs)\n\nActually, I'm getting confused by the preemption logic. Let me just try a practical change.\n\nLet me reorder the `global_strategy` to move `fight2` earlier, before the eating strategies. This way, the bot will fight monsters more frequently, gaining experience and progressing deeper.\n\nLet me try this change:","time":{"start":1790469453444,"end":1790469463159}}}
