{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790469758814,"sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","part":{"id":"prt_0e04f7b510011M2WWWwgmaJ2AL","messageID":"msg_0e04f766f001QZBjHWEFtY5srk","sessionID":"ses_f1fc909e3ffenldWD8cipIdpHJ","type":"reasoning","text":"Wait, I need to reconsider this. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` is True for SATIATED (0) and NOT_HUNGRY (1), meaning \"don't eat\". The bot eats when `hunger_state >= Hunger.HUNGRY`, i.e., HUNGRY (2), WEAK (3), FAINTING (4).\n\nIf I change to `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`, this is True for SATIATED (0 <= 1) and NOT_HUNGRY (1 <= 1), meaning \"don't eat\" would be... wait, no. The `yield False` means \"don't execute the strategy body\", which means \"don't eat\". So:\n\nCurrent: `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` → yield False (don't eat) when SATIATED or NOT_HUNGRY; eat when HUNGRY+/wanted\n\nNew: `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` → yield False (don't eat) when SATIATED (0 <= 1) or NOT_HUNGRY (1 <= 1); eat when HUNGRY (2 <= 1 is False) or WEAK/FAINTING\n\nWait, that's the same as the current behavior! `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` is the same as `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` when the enum values are SATIATED=0, NOT_HUNGRY=1, HUNGRY=2, WEAK=3, FAINTING=4.\n\nLet me verify:\n- `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`: SATIATED (0 < 2) → True; NOT_HUNGRY (1 < 2) → True; HUNGRY (2 < 2) → False; WEAK (3 < 2) → False; FAINTING (4 < 2) → False\n- `hunger_state <= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`: SATIATED (0 <= 1) → True; NOT_HUNGRY (1 <= 1) → True; HUNGRY (2 <= 1) → False; WEAK (3 <= 1) → False; FAINTING (4 <= 1) → False\n\nYes, they're the same! I didn't actually change the behavior. Let me think of a different modification.\n\nWhat if I change to `hunger_state < Hunger.FAINING`? This would be:\n- SATIATED (0 < 4) → True → don't eat\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1 < 4) → True → don't eat\n- HUNGRY (2 < 4) → True → don't eat\n- WEAK (3 < 4) → True → don't eat\n- FAINTING (4 < 4) → False → eat\n\nThis would make the bot eat ONLY when FAINTING. That's much less frequent than the current behavior. Bad.\n\nWhat about `hunger_state < Hunger.WEAK`?\n- SATIATED (0 < 3) → True → don't eat\n- NOT_HUNGRY (1 < 3) → True → don't eat\n- HUNGRY (2 < 3) → True → don't eat\n- WEAK (3 < 3) → False → eat\n- FAINTING (4 < 3) → False → eat\n\nThis would make the bot eat only when WEAK or FAINTING. That's less frequent than the current behavior (which eats when HUNGRY+, i.e., HUNGRY, WEAK, FAINTING). Bad.\n\nHmm, so all the simple condition changes either maintain the same behavior or make it worse. The current condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` is already reasonable - the bot eats when HUNGRY or worse.\n\nThe issue must be elsewhere. Let me reconsider.\n\nMaybe the problem is that the bot isn't finding enough food, not that the eating condition is wrong. The `eat_corpses_from_ground` strategy should provide food from corpses on the ground, but maybe there aren't enough corpses, or the `_is_corpse_editable` check is too restrictive.\n\nLet me look at the `_is_corpse_editable` check for poison again:\n```python\nif self.character.race != Character.ORC and permonst.mflags1 & MON.M1_POIS != 0:\n    return False\n```\n\nThis rejects poisonous corpses for all non-orc races. Dwarves have innate poison resistance, so this check is overly restrictive for them. But the problem says not to tune to particular races.\n\nHowever, this check affects ALL non-orc races, not just dwarves. Humans, elves, gnomes would also be affected. So modifying this to be less restrictive could be a general improvement.\n\nBut wait, in NetHack, eating poisonous corpses can give you poison, which is dangerous. The game has mechanics for dealing with poison (antidote, poison resistance). The current check might be too conservative, but removing it entirely could cause the bot to die from poisoning.\n\nLet me try a different modification. What if I modify the `_is_corpse_editable` to allow eating poisonous corpses when the bot has the required intrinsics or when the corpse is not too dangerous? But this would require adding intrinsics tracking, which is complex.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. Let me look at the `eat_corpses_from_ground` strategy's condition more carefully. The condition is `hunger_state >= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`, which as I analyzed, means the bot eats ground corpses when NOT satiated (NOT_HUNGRY, HUNGRY, WEAK, FAINTING) and doesn't eat when SATIATED.\n\nBut the `eat_from_inventory` strategy runs every 5 turns with condition `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY`, which means it doesn't eat when SATIATED or NOT_HUNGRY, and does eat when HUNGRY, WEAK, FAINTING.\n\nSo the combined behavior is:\n- SATIATED: neither strategy eats → bot stays satiated\n- NOT_HUNGRY: `eat_from_inventory` doesn't eat, `eat_corpses_from_ground` does eat\n- HUNGRY: both strategies eat\n- WEAK: both strategies eat\n- FAINTING: both strategies eat\n\nThe issue might be that when the bot is SATIATED, neither strategy eats. The bot needs to become NOT_HUNGRY first (by taking actions that decrease hunger), and then HUNGRY before `eat_from_inventory` kicks in. If the bot takes too many actions without becoming hungry, or if the hunger decrease rate is slow, the bot might not eat until it's already weak.\n\nActually, in NetHack, hunger decreases as you take actions. The rate depends on what you're doing. Moving decreases hunger slowly, fighting decreases it more, etc. So the bot should naturally become hungry over time.\n\nBut the starvation deaths at Xp:7 suggest the bot dies before it can eat. Maybe the bot is taking too many turns exploring without finding food, or the hunger state management has a bug.\n\nLet me try a completely different change. What if I modify the `explore1` function to prioritize food items? The `to_visit_func` and `to_search_func` determine which tiles the bot visits. If I add a priority for food objects, the bot would more frequently find and pick up food.\n\nLet me look at the `to_visit_func` and `to_search_func` priority calculations again. The priorities are based on:\n- `to_visit_func`: stones and doors\n- `to_search_func`: search count penalty, doors, stones, walkability, distance\n\nI could add a priority for food objects (FOOD_CLASS glyphs) in the `to_search_func`. This would cause the bot to more frequently visit tiles with food, pick them up, and eat them.\n\nLet me implement this change. I'll add a food priority in the `to_search_func` function.\n\nActually, wait. The `explore1` function explores the level and the bot can pick up items via `gather_items` → `pickup_and_drop_items` → `arrange_items`. The bot picks up items from the ground, and then the `eat_from_inventory` strategy eats from inventory. So if the bot picks up more food, it should eat more.\n\nBut the `gather_items` strategy might not prioritize food. Let me check the `ItemPriority._split` method in `global_logic.py` to see how food is prioritized.\n\nLooking at the `ItemPriority._split` method (which I read earlier), it has various prioritization logic:\n- Takes sacrificial corpses if `_take_sacrificial_corpses` is set\n- Takes best melee weapon\n- Takes best armor set\n- Takes thrown projectiles\n- Takes food (comestibles) with nutrition priority\n- For lawful characters, takes long swords\n- Takes thrown projectiles\n- Takes food (not corpses) with nutrition per weight priority\n- Takes unknown items, etc.\n- Takes weapons, armor, tools, food, gems, rings, wands, scrolls, spellbooks\n\nSo food IS prioritized, but maybe not highly enough. The food prioritization is:\n```python\nfor item in sorted(filter(lambda i: i.is_food() and not i.is_corpse(), items),\n                   key=lambda x: -x.nutrition_per_weight() - 1000 * (x.objs[0].name == 'sprig of wolfsbane')):\n    add_item(item)\n```\n\nThis sorts food by nutrition per weight (high to low), with sprig of wolfsbane getting a very negative priority (avoided). So the bot prioritizes high-nutrition food, which is correct.\n\nBut the issue might be that the bot isn't finding enough food on the ground, or the food it finds is low-nutrition.\n\nOK, let me try modifying the `explore1` function to give a priority boost for food objects. This would cause the bot to more frequently visit tiles with food, pick them up, and ultimately eat them.\n\nI'll add a food priority in the `to_search_func` function, similar to how stones and doors are prioritized. The priority would be based on the food's nutrition per weight.\n\nLet me implement this change.","time":{"start":1790469700433,"end":1790469758806}}}
